"An abstract syntax tree represents the semantic syntactic structure of source code without retaining every punctuation token.\n\nUse when: Explain a compiler transformation or code-analysis rule. Inspect expression nesting and operator precedence.\n\nAvoid when: Exact source formatting or runtime execution is the subject.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes denote language constructs such as calls, declarations, and binary expressions. Parent-child links mean syntactic containment. Labeled child roles distinguish operands, conditions, names, and bodies.\n\nBuild: Choose a short source fragment and its language version. Obtain the parsed structure from the actual compiler where possible. Keep meaningful node kinds and annotate source spans only when needed. Lay out parents above ordered children with ample space for role labels.\n\nCheck: The root and child order agree with the parsed source. Operator precedence is represented by nesting rather than guessed from spacing. Removed tokens are not silently treated as semantic nodes. Sibling labels and connectors remain separate at every level.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing an AST with a concrete parse tree. Inventing node kinds that the selected compiler does not expose.\n\nExample: The expression total + tax * rate has an addition root whose right child is a multiplication expression.\n\nAlternatives: Railroad diagram, Control-flow graph."
